[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Zheng et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d4a78af211768d1513d66ec28fe0596218300f22)
- venue: Nature Machine Intelligence
- citation count: 301
[method]
The paper evaluates DrugVQA, a classical deep-learning drug-target interaction predictor that is related to the proposed NCGAMI framework at the level of the overall research problem, but not at the level of non-commutative geometry, quantum optimization, or statistical-manifold adaptation. DrugVQA encodes protein distance maps with a dynamic attentive CNN built from residual blocks and attention MLPs, encodes drug SMILES strings with a self-attentive BiLSTM, and combines the resulting representations with a classifier to predict interactions. A sequence-based variant replaces the protein distance map with protein sequences processed by the same self-attentive BiLSTM. The model was implemented in PyTorch 0.4.0 and evaluated on the DUD-E, Human, and BindingDB datasets using AUC, precision, recall, ROC enrichment, and accuracy. This method is relevant to the NCGAMI research plan because it represents the class of attention-based, classically optimized DTI predictors that the plan positions as limited by classical probability, Euclidean representations, and standard deep-learning training, whereas NCGAMI proposes non-commutative geometric, spectral-action, and quantum-adiabatic components.
[results]
On the Human dataset, DrugVQA outperformed its protein-sequence variant by 1.9% in AUC, 3.3% in recall, and 4.4% in precision, and was comparable to a GNN baseline. Descriptor-based machine learning methods achieved lower performance, with AUC values ranging from 0.86 to 0.94. On DUD-E, DrugVQA achieved an average AUC of 0.971 compared with 0.94 for the GNN baseline and outperformed 3D-CNN on 96% of targets, suggesting that the 2D distance-map attention representation was more effective than the tested 3D-CNN and GNN approaches. On BindingDB, DrugVQA performed consistently across seen and unseen proteins, while Tiresias, DBN, and GNN showed stronger degradation on unseen proteins. E2E was more stable than those baselines but remained lower than DrugVQA by 2.6% in AUC and 2.3% in accuracy on average. These outcomes provide a performance reference for classical attention-based DTI methods that the NCGAMI plan would need to exceed, although they do not validate the non-commutative or quantum components proposed in the research plan.
[context]
The experimental setup used training for at most 50 epochs with the Adam optimizer at a learning rate of 0.001 and batch size 1. The model used L2 regularization with coefficient 0.001, dropout of 0.2 on the BiLSTM and self-attention MLP units, a BiLSTM hidden state of 64, attention MLP hidden layers with 100 units, protein matrix embeddings with 10 rows, drug matrix embeddings with 18 rows, and 30 residual blocks with 16 and 32 filters due to the memory limitation of a GTX1080Ti 12GB GPU. The Human dataset was evaluated with AUC, precision, and recall; DUD-E was evaluated with AUC and ROC enrichment at 0.5%, 1%, 2%, and 5% false positive rate thresholds; BindingDB was evaluated with AUC and accuracy. This context is relevant to the NCGAMI research plan because the plan also targets the Human dataset and uses AUC-based evaluation, but it proposes a substantially different implementation using distributed A100 GPUs and quantum-geometric optimization, making DrugVQA a useful classical baseline for assessing whether the proposed non-commutative and quantum components add predictive value.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Bauer et al., 2020-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e4fc036bf2ab52e10390df0891041cbf451fda63)
- venue: Chemical Reviews
- citation count: 992
[method]
The paper discusses hybrid quantum-classical algorithms for molecular and materials simulation, in which a quantum device performs a quantum optimization, state-preparation, or simulation step while a classical device coordinates the workflow. The most relevant component is the use of quantum optimizers and quantum annealers to assist a classical conformational search, which is conceptually related to the proposed NCGAMI framework’s quantum adiabatic optimization step for minimizing a non-commutative geometric variational objective. The paper also mentions variational quantum eigensolver-style workflows and classical post-processing of quantum eigenstates, but it does not describe a drug-target interaction dataset, a non-commutative pharmacological manifold, spectral-action domain adaptation, or Fisher-Rao optimization.
[results]
No quantitative experimental outcomes, baseline comparisons, AUC or AUPR values, or statistical significance are reported in the provided excerpt for the hybrid quantum-classical optimization method. The excerpt only states that quantum optimizers and quantum annealers can be viewed as quantum-classical hybrids and that a proof-of-principle demonstration of a related quantum impurity Green’s function algorithm was proposed, without numerical performance data.
[context]
The context is a review of quantum simulation challenges rather than an empirical drug-target interaction study. It provides no dataset size, hardware model, qubit count, gate depth, hyperparameters, or evaluation metrics for the quantum optimizer or annealer example. The only scale mentioned is that a small quantum computer with a few hundred qubits was suggested for DMFT-based material simulations, but this is not tied to the proposed NCGAMI experiment design or DTI prediction benchmarks.
[end related work 2]

[start related work 3]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Abbasi et al., 2020-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ad671537693f3d2f2a0b19d7a48157d772cb7bb3)
- venue: Bioinform.
- citation count: 271
[method]
The paper evaluates a classical drug-target interaction prediction model, DeepCDA, that combines embedding layers, convolutional and LSTM feature encoders for protein sequences and compound representations, a two-sided attention mechanism, and adversarial domain adaptation to transfer knowledge between datasets. This is related to the proposed NCGAMI framework because both target drug-target interaction prediction and domain adaptation, and the paper’s baselines and AUPR metric overlap with the planned NCGAMI evaluation; however, the paper does not implement NCGAMI’s non-commutative geometric manifold, spectral action objective, quantum adiabatic optimization, or Fisher-Rao statistical manifold. The experiments had two aims: testing the CNN-LSTM feature encoder and attention, and assessing cross-dataset generalization. KIBA and Davis used nested cross-validation with six equal parts, one held-out test part and five-fold training validation, averaging five results; BindingDB used a fixed partition with four protein classes excluded from training. The proposed model and baselines used similar training and test partitions for fair comparison. Baselines included KronRLS, SimBoost, DeepAffinity, and DeepDTA, with most baseline results taken from original publications unless trained by the authors. Continuous predictions were evaluated with CI, MSE, r2m, and Pearson correlation, and binary predictions were evaluated with AUPR after applying dataset-specific thresholds.
[results]
On KIBA, the full model outperformed the listed baselines on all reported metrics. CI improved by 0.026, MSE was 0.018 lower than DeepDTA, and AUPR improved by about 0.024 over the best comparable approach. The attention-related comparison reported a 0.014 CI improvement. A paired t-test at the 0.05 significance level showed superiority over DeepDTA with p < 0.0001. On Davis, the full model outperformed baselines on all reported metrics. The paper reports improvements over DeepDTA of 0.013, 0.019, and 0.025 associated with CI, r2m, and AUPR, and reports an r2m value of 0.248. The LSTM-only variant had CI 0.014 lower than DeepDTA, while the attention variant had CI 0.005 higher than DeepDTA. A paired t-test showed superiority over DeepDTA with p around 0.001. On BindingDB, the full model achieved a higher Pearson correlation coefficient than DeepAffinity and DeepDTA; CNN-LSTM outperformed CNN-only and LSTM-only encoders; the full model with domain adaptation improved all metrics over the DeepCDA+w.o. Attention variant; and a paired t-test showed superiority over DeepDTA with p around 0.01.
[context]
KIBA contained 467 proteins, 52,498 compounds, and 246,088 interactions before filtering out proteins and compounds with fewer than 10 interactions. Davis contained 442 proteins and 68 compounds, with Kd values transformed into log space. BindingDB used Ki-labelled samples, with 101,134 training and 43,391 test samples after excluding nuclear estrogen receptors with 516 samples, ion channels with 8,101 samples, receptor tyrosine kinases with 3,355 samples, and G-protein-coupled receptors with 77,994 samples from training. Imbalance ratios were 0.082 for Davis, 0.192 for KIBA, and 0.349 for BindingDB. Binary thresholds were 7 for Davis, 12.1 for KIBA, and 7.6 for BindingDB. Hyperparameter search included filter counts of 16, 32, 64, 128, and 256; compound and protein filter lengths; feature encoder learning rates of 0.1, 0.01, 0.001, and 0.0001; test feature encoder learning rates of 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, and 0.00001; and discriminator learning rates of 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, and 0.00001. The implementation used Python 3.6, TensorFlow, Keras, an Intel Core i7-7700HQ CPU, an NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1070 8GB GPU, 32GB RAM, minibatch size 256, and about 0.01 second per training iteration. This provides a classical single-GPU baseline and evaluation protocol for the NCGAMI plan, which instead targets DrugBank and a Human dataset with 6:4 source-target splits, AUC/AUPR, ablations, and eight A100 GPUs.
