[method]
The paper develops a C*-algebraic quantum tomography and reconstruction framework. Starting from a unital C*-algebra A and a state rho, it uses the GNS construction to build a Hilbert space H_rho as the completion of the quotient A/J_rho, where J_rho is the null ideal of elements a with rho(a*a)=0. The construction yields a cyclic representation pi_rho of A on H_rho, a vacuum vector corresponding to the unit, vector states, and more general normal states described by density operators on H_rho. The paper then defines a tomographic map U from a parameter space M into A and a dual tomographic map D from M into the dual space A'. A pair U,D is called a normalized tomographic pair when D has square-integrable norm and its kernel kappa(y,x)=<D(x),U(y)> satisfies a reproducing condition for sampling functions. The reconstruction theorem proves that the associated reconstruction map F_D is a left inverse of the sampling map F_U, so the original state rho can be recovered from its sampling function. The paper further defines a quantum Radon transform associated with a Lie group dynamical system: for a representation of a Lie group G on A and a fiducial state rho, one-parameter unitary groups generated by elements of the Lie algebra produce self-adjoint operators on H_rho. Spectral measures of these operators define quantum tomograms, and when the measures are absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure, they yield nonnegative density functions. This method relates to the proposed NCGAMI method because the proposed non-commutative pharmacological manifold also begins with a C*-algebra A of observables and a Hilbert space H, and the paper provides a rigorous template for constructing H from A and for representing and reconstructing quantum states. However, the paper does not define an unbounded self-adjoint Dirac operator, a spectral action, or any drug-target interaction prediction algorithm.
