The paper uses a Procrustes-in-Wasserstein-distance method to learn an orthogonal transformation and one-to-one correspondences between two point sets when neither the transformation nor the matching is known. The formulation combines orthogonal Procrustes, which has a closed-form solution when correspondences are fixed, with a Wasserstein/assignment objective, which has an exact solution when the transformation is fixed. Because the joint problem is nonconvex and naive alternating minimization is reported to converge to poor local minima, the paper instead uses a stochastic optimization procedure. The stochastic procedure is evaluated under two initialization strategies: random initialization and initialization from the solution of a convex relaxation. In the toy experiments, the inputs are English word embeddings trained with fastText skip-gram and CBoW models on 100 million tokens from the 2007 News Crawl corpus, and only the first 10,000 vectors are used. This method is related to the proposed research plan because both concern domain adaptation through learning correspondences or transport-like mappings between domains, but the paper’s method is a classical Wasserstein/Procrustes alignment of word-embedding point clouds rather than a non-commutative spectral-action functional for pharmacological drug-target interaction domains.
