[method]
The paper uses an invariance-aware optimal transport space-alignment method. It represents source and target samples as matrices X and Y in R^{d x n}, seeks a transformation T from a class F including orthogonal Procrustes and l2 or l-infinity global geometric invariances, and optimizes a transport objective over both the transformation and the transport coupling. In paired or semi-supervised settings, Procrustes alignment is solved through singular value decomposition; in unsupervised correspondence recovery, the method optimizes an entropy-regularized transport problem with global invariances and extracts discrete correspondences by taking the argmax of the coupling matrix. It is evaluated on synthetic 3D point clouds with known transformations and on unsupervised word translation using fastText embeddings and 5K cross-lingual dictionaries. This relates to the proposed NCGAMI method because both aim to learn domain-adaptive transport maps between source and target domains, but the paper uses classical Euclidean optimal transport and rigid or global geometric invariances rather than non-commutative spectral action, quantum adiabatic optimization, or pharmacological drug-target interaction modeling.
[results]
On synthetic point clouds, l-infinity invariant optimal transport recovered the correct correspondences when the true transformation was orthogonal, whereas classic invariance-agnostic optimal transport failed and matched greedily by proximity. Under Gaussian noise, l-infinity invariant transport outperformed l2 invariant transport when the true transformation was orthogonal, and the reverse held for the other invariance family. Both invariant transport methods outperformed an oracle entropy-regularized transport method that did not model the transformation in the l-infinity case, which the authors attribute to the freedom to choose the transformation to absorb noise and to easier optimization over the l-infinity transformation family. The l2 case showed a performance gap relative to the oracle due to estimation error in the transformation. In word translation, the framework performed on par with state-of-the-art unsupervised methods at a fraction of the computational cost, and the unsupervised objective was strongly correlated with translation accuracy, enabling model selection without labels. The provided excerpt does not report exact accuracy values, AUC, AUPR, or statistical significance.
[context]
Synthetic experiments used point clouds in R3, transformations sampled from invariance families, discrete matchings extracted from couplings, and accuracy measured against known true point-wise correspondences. Noisy experiments added Gaussian noise with variance sigma to target points and reported mean and one standard deviation over 5 repetitions. Word-translation experiments used fastText embeddings and parallel dictionaries for 110 language pairs, focused on English-Spanish, English-French, English-Italian, English-German, and English-Russian, included a 5K-pair semi-supervised Procrustes baseline, compared against unsupervised baselines including SelfLearn, Gromov-Wasserstein, adversarial, and Wasserstein methods, and used cross-domain similarity local scaling for nearest-neighbor search. Hardware, exact hyperparameters, and runtime details were not specified in the provided excerpt, and the method is not directly comparable to the proposed non-commutative, quantum, or drug-target-specific NCGAMI framework.
