[method]
The paper studies domain adaptation under covariate shift and generalized target shift by learning a representation and classifier while minimizing a Wasserstein distance between source and target marginal distributions in a latent space. For generalized target shift, it uses a match-and-reweight strategy: target class proportions are estimated, either by hierarchical clustering or Gaussian mixtures in the MARS variants, and are used to form a target-proportion-weighted source distribution and a target distribution. An optimal coupling or assignment is then used to align class-conditionals and reweight source samples. The theoretical part shows that, under geometric ordering assumptions on the class-conditionals, zero Wasserstein distance between the weighted marginals implies zero distance between the corresponding class-conditionals. Experiments compare a source-only model, an adversarial domain adaptation baseline, a standard Wasserstein baseline, a relaxed Wasserstein variant, an importance-weighted Wasserstein method, and the MARS variants on a toy three-class Gaussian dataset and on real visual domain adaptation benchmarks involving digits, VisDA, and office data. Evaluation uses balanced accuracy and averages over 20 runs.
[results]
On the toy dataset with fixed mean shift and varying label imbalance, the adversarial baseline degrades as imbalance increases. The standard Wasserstein baseline performs well across imbalance levels but requires a larger relaxation or distance parameter as imbalance grows. The importance-weighted Wasserstein method fails because the shift in class-conditional distributions prevents proper estimation of importance weights. The MARS variants are adaptive to imbalance and perform well over a broad range in both low-noise and mid-noise settings. In the hardest low-dimensional toy case, all methods struggle and reach only about 0.67 balanced accuracy over some imbalance range. The paper reports that, for this low-dimensional toy problem, the Gaussian-mixture and optimal-transport-based matching variant achieves the best performance, but the provided excerpt does not include a full quantitative table, per-method accuracy values, or statistical significance tests.
