[method]
The paper uses a classical optimal-transport domain-adaptation procedure based on measure pre-conditioning and a conditional average guess. A convolutional neural network is trained only on MNIST digits 1 and 9 with sparse cross-entropy loss to classify whether an input is a 1 or a 9. The authors then assume that the source class 1 is transferable to the target class 7 and that the source class 9 is transferable to the target class 6. Using the POT Python library, they compute empirical optimal transport matchings between source and target conditional samples for the 1-to-7 pair and the 6-to-9 pair, using the cost function c(x, y) = |x - y|. As a pre-conditioning step, every target 6 is flipped so that it more closely resembles a 9 before transport. The final adapted agent is constructed by applying the conditional average guess: for each target label, the source model prediction is transported through the estimated optimal transport map and then averaged over the target conditional samples. Because the POT library provides only sample matchings rather than a full continuous transport map, the continuous map is approximated by averaging over the matched conditional samples. This method is related to the proposed research method because it also seeks domain adaptation by constructing transport maps between source and target domains, but it is a classical empirical optimal-transport and pre-conditioning approach and does not use the spectral action principle, non-commutative geometry, quantum relative entropy, or quantum adiabatic optimization described in the research plan.
[results]
