The paper implements a variational quantum adiabatic algorithm, VQAA, on a Rydberg-atom array to solve the maximum independent set problem. The quantum objective is a Rydberg Hamiltonian whose ground state encodes an independent-set solution, and the algorithm optimizes a quasi-adiabatic detuning path Δ(t) from negative to positive values at constant Rabi coupling Ω. The detuning profile is piecewise linear with f segments and is parameterized by segment durations τ_i, segment end detunings Δ_i, an initial detuning Δ0, a low-pass filter time constant τΔ, and a turn-on/off time τΩ, giving 2f+3 variational parameters. In the reported VQAA run, f=3, so 9 parameters were optimized. A classical optimizer updates these parameters using a figure of merit obtained from the quantum device; each parameter set is evaluated by running the quantum machine typically 50 times on a target graph, performing single-site fluorescence readout, and applying post-processing such as vertex reduction to remove blockade violations and enforce the independent-set constraint. The paper also compares VQAA with QAOA, where resonant pulses Ω(t), φ(t), and Δ(t) are optimized for depths p=1 to p=5. This method relates to the proposed NCGAMI research plan because both use a quantum adiabatic or variational quantum optimization strategy to minimize a variational objective, but the paper demonstrates the approach on a physical quantum hardware objective, maximum independent set on Rydberg arrays, rather than on non-commutative geometric drug-target interaction prediction.
[results]
The VQAA optimization started from a purely linear detuning sweep and produced an optimized piecewise-linear sweep for f=3 segments. For time-scaling experiments, the pulse shape was optimized for a total sweep duration T=1.25 µs, corresponding to p=10, and then rescaled to other sweep durations while keeping the turn-on/off time τΩ fixed. Increasing the number of linear segments produced sweep shapes similar to the three-segment sweep and performance comparable to the three-segment case. In the QAOA comparison, depth p=1 used a single variable-duration pulse, p=2 used three parameters optimized by brute-force direct search, and p=3 to p=5 used a classical optimizer; the classical optimizer gave comparable results with fewer quantum queries. QAOA performance saturated beyond p=4 and remained far below the quasi-adiabatic VQAA performance. Numerical simulations on a 24-vertex graph showed that finite next-nearest-neighbor interactions, V_NNN/2π=13 MHz relative to Ω/2π=4.0 MHz, caused blockade violations and leakage out of the independent-set subspace, worsening QAOA performance even when parameters were re-optimized for the finite-interaction case. The supplied excerpt does not provide absolute approximation-error values or statistical significance for the VQAA curves, so the quantitative outcome is mainly the parameter count, sweep duration, shot count, and relative performance comparisons. Unlike the proposed method, which states a convergence guarantee under specified conditions, the paper’s VQAA is a heuristic variational search on noisy hardware and does not provide a formal convergence guarantee.
[context]
The experiment used a Rydberg-atom array with fluorescence imaging: atoms in |0> were detected by fluorescence, while atoms in |1> were inferred from absence of fluorescence and were not distinguishable from atom loss. Some readout images contained more |1> atoms than the MIS limit, attributed to detection errors, finite blockade violations, quantum fluctuations, and possible Rydberg antiblockade. The hardware had a two-photon Rabi frequency Ω/2π=4.0 MHz and next-nearest-neighbor interaction V_NNN/2π=13 MHz; QAOA pulses were implemented with an acousto-optic modulator, where phase changes caused wavefront discontinuities and brief intensity reductions of about 10 ns. The VQAA used a constant Rabi coupling and a piecewise-linear detuning profile with turn-on/off ramps and a low-pass filter to suppress excitations. The reported VQAA time-scaling pulse was optimized at T=1.25 µs, corresponding to p=10, and rescaled while keeping τΩ constant. Graph characterization used tensor-network calculations of MIS degeneracy D_|MIS| and D_|MIS|-1 for random graphs up to N=1095 at 80% filling of a 37×37 lattice, with 1000 graphs generated per size; worst-case degeneracy and hardness parameter appeared to scale exponentially with N. This context is relevant to the proposed NCGAMI plan because it shows that quantum adiabatic optimization can be made practical with a small variational parameterization, but convergence and performance are strongly affected by hardware noise, measurement budget, and the choice of adiabatic path, which should be considered when designing a quantum adiabatic solver for the non-commutative spectral-geometry objective.
