The paper uses a domain-adaptation benchmarking protocol on visual datasets, most directly the single-source DomainNet evaluation. It constructs DomainNet with six visual domains and 345 object categories, then tests each of the 30 source-target domain pairs by training on one source domain and evaluating on the target domain. Baselines include DAN, JAN, DANN, RTN, ADDA, MCD, and SE, using AlexNet, ResNet-101, or ResNet-152 backbones according to the original method settings. The paper also evaluates its multi-source moment-matching model, M3SDA and M3SDA-beta, on digit recognition and Office-Caltech10 by rotating target domains and combining source domains. This relates to the proposed NCGAMI DTI study at the level of experimental design: both partition data into source and target domains, create target-domain train/test splits, and compare an adaptation framework against established baselines, but the paper's task is visual domain adaptation rather than drug-target interaction prediction.
