For DomainNet single-source adaptation, the supplied text reports that the dataset is challenging, especially for infograph and quickdraw domains, but does not provide the full numeric table for all 30 source-target combinations. In the paper's broader domain-adaptation experiments, M3SDA achieved 86.13% average accuracy on digit recognition and M3SDA-beta achieved 87.65%, outperforming the listed discrepancy- and adversarial-based baselines. On Office-Caltech10, M3SDA achieved 96.1% average accuracy and M3SDA-beta achieved 96.4%, using ResNet-101 pre-trained on ImageNet. These values are visual recognition accuracies and are not directly comparable to the proposed DTI AUC or AUPR metrics.
[context]
DomainNet contains six domains: clipart, infograph, painting, quickdraw, real, and sketch, with 345 categories and 423.5k cleaned images after manual filtering of 1.2M crawled images; quickdraw contributes 172.5k images with 500 per category. The single-source DomainNet experiments use a 70/30 train/test split for each domain and 30 source-target combinations. The overall experimental suite includes 714 experiments run on a 24-GPU cluster for more than 21,440 GPU-hours, implemented in PyTorch 3, with trade-off parameter lambda set to 0.5 and observed insensitivity between 0.1 and 1. Digit experiments sample 25,000 training and 9,000 testing images from MNIST, MNIST-M, SVHN, and Synthetic Digits, use the full 9,298-image USPS set, and report five-run means and standard deviations. Office-Caltech10 contains 10 categories across Amazon, Caltech, DSLR, and Webcam domains.
