- in_text_citation: [(Gururangan et al., 2020-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e816f788767eec6a8ef0ea9eddd0e902435d4271)
- venue: Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics
- citation count: 3368
[method]
The paper uses domain-adaptive pretraining as a target-domain adaptation method for a transformer language model. An off-the-shelf ROBERTA-base source model is continued pretraining on a target-domain corpus for 12.5K steps, corresponding to one pass over each domain dataset, and is then fine-tuned on a target classification task by passing the final [CLS] representation to a task-specific feed-forward layer. The target domains are BIOMED, CS, NEWS, and REVIEWS, with eight tasks: CHEMPROT, RCT, ACL-ARC, SCIERC, HYPERPARTISAN, AGNEWS, HELPFULNESS, and IMDB. The evaluation compares three settings: source-only ROBERTA fine-tuning, domain-adaptive pretraining on the matching target domain, and domain-adaptive pretraining on an irrelevant domain as a control. This relates to the proposed NCGAMI evaluation because both organize the problem into source and target domains and test whether adapting the model to the target domain improves target-domain prediction. The paper’s domain-adaptive pretraining provides a directly analogous baseline and control design for the proposed 6:4 source/target partition of DrugBank and Human drug-target interaction data, where the key question is whether target-domain adaptation improves DTI prediction beyond a source-only model.
[results]
Domain-adaptive pretraining improved or matched the source-only ROBERTA baseline on all eight tasks, with the largest gains in domains farther from the source corpus. ACL-ARC improved from 63.0 to 75.4 macro-F1, SCIERC from 77.3 to 80.8 macro-F1, CHEMPROT from 81.9 to 84.2 micro-F1, HYPERPARTISAN from 86.6 to 88.2 macro-F1, HELPFULNESS from 65.1 to 66.5 macro-F1, IMDB from 95.0 to 95.4 macro-F1, and RCT from 87.2 to 87.6 micro-F1, while AGNEWS remained at 93.9 macro-F1. Irrelevant-domain adaptation was generally worse than matching-domain adaptation, for example HYPERPARTISAN dropped to 76.4 compared with 88.2 for matching-domain adaptation, ACL-ARC dropped to 66.4 compared with 75.4, CHEMPROT dropped to 79.4 compared with 84.2, SCIERC dropped to 79.2 compared with 80.8, IMDB dropped to 94.1 compared with 95.4, HELPFULNESS dropped to 65.1 compared with 66.5, RCT dropped to 86.9 compared with 87.6, and AGNEWS dropped to 93.5 compared with 93.9. Results are reported as averages across five random seeds, with standard deviations reported in the paper.
[context]
The experiments used ROBERTA-base, a v3-8 TPU, 12.5K domain-adaptive pretraining steps per domain, and task-specific feed-forward classification heads. The source model was pretrained on a general web corpus, and the target domains were biomedical, computer science, news, and reviews. The tasks spanned low- and high-resource settings, with labeled training sizes ranging from 515 examples for HYPERPARTISAN to 115,251 examples for HELPFULNESS, and unlabeled domain data ranging from 114 examples for ACL-ARC to 50,000 examples for several high-resource tasks. This context is relevant to the proposed DTI study because it shows that domain-adaptation benefits depend on target-domain relevance, task resource level, and whether the adaptation domain is aligned with the target task. For the proposed NCGAMI evaluation on DrugBank and the Human dataset, the 6:4 source/target split and target train/test subdivision should be paired with source-only, target-adapted, and irrelevant-domain controls, multiple random seeds, and AUC/AUPR reporting to determine whether the non-commutative spectral adaptation produces gains beyond standard domain adaptation.
[end related work 4]

