[context]
BindingDB S contains 59,136 interactions involving 48,084 drugs and 798 targets, split into training, validation, and test sets; entries exceeding DeepAffinity length constraints are removed. BindingDB L contains 472,925 interactions involving 321,950 drugs and 3,350 targets, with training, validation, test, and four out-of-distribution protein-family test sets for ion channels, nuclear estrogen receptors, G-protein-coupled receptors, and receptor tyrosine kinases. For BindingDB L, a validation set is created by randomly selecting 10% of training samples. DeepAffinity is treated as a regressor with classification thresholds of 0.5 for BindingDB S and 6 for BindingDB L, uses target four-mers, SMILES-derived drug alphabet, pre-trained embeddings, and accepts targets up to 1,500 amino acids and SMILES up to 100 symbols. DeepConv-DTI trains protein embeddings from scratch, uses Morgan fingerprints of radius 2 for drugs, and accepts targets up to 2,500 amino acids. TransformerCPI uses overlapping protein three-mers with a pre-trained word2vec embedding and RDKit-derived 34 atom properties for drugs. Random Forest uses 2,500-dimensional label-encoded amino acid vectors and Morgan fingerprints of radius 2. No hyperparameter optimization is performed, and hardware specifications are not reported. These conditions are relevant to the proposed NCGAMI experiments as a DTI benchmarking context, but they do not use DrugBank, the Human dataset, or the proposed 6:4 source-target split.
