[method]
The paper evaluates an end-to-end deep learning drug-target interaction predictor by constructing a binary classification benchmark from a BindingDB snapshot and comparing it against matrix factorization, a similarity-based predictor, and a deep learning baseline that uses expert-designed molecular fingerprints and protein descriptors. The proposed model learns directly from SMILES chemical structures, protein amino acid sequences, and Gene Ontology annotations, with an ablated version without Gene Ontology annotations. The evaluation follows four entity-visibility settings based on whether the test protein and drug appear in training, and the systems are allocated to training, development, and test sets. This is related to the proposed NCGAMI benchmarking method because it is a DTI predictor comparison protocol focused on generalization to unseen proteins and drugs, but it does not include DeepDTA, DeepConv-DTI, MolTrans, TransformerCPI, non-commutative geometry, or quantum optimization.
[results]
The end-to-end model was the only method that consistently achieved values greater than 0.8 across all four test settings and all reported metrics. When the four settings were aggregated, it outperformed all baselines on all metrics, with an average AUPR of 0.91 compared with 0.81 for the deep learning baseline. Baselines performed better than the end-to-end model when tested proteins were observed in training, but their performance dropped sharply when tested proteins were unobserved; the similarity-based method failed in that condition, and the deep learning baseline was generally worse than the end-to-end model without Gene Ontology annotations except for accuracy in the third setting.
[context]
The dataset was built from 1.3 million BindingDB records and filtered to 39,747 positive and 31,218 negative examples using PubChem CID with SMILES, UniProt sequence and Gene Ontology annotations, IC50 values, molecular weight below 1,000 Da, positive IC50 below 100 nM, and negative IC50 above 10,000 nM. Matrix factorization was evaluated only in the setting where both protein and drug were observed in training and used additional raw IC50 examples outside the classification range. The similarity-based method used expert-designed protein and drug similarity measures, while the deep learning baseline used middle-level expert features. No hardware, hyperparameters, or statistical significance tests are reported. This context is relevant to the proposed research plan because it demonstrates the importance of entity-visibility splits and content-based representations for DTI generalization, but it uses different datasets, baselines, and evaluation scope than the proposed NCGAMI study.
