- venue: Nature Machine Intelligence
- citation count: 301
[method]
The paper evaluated DrugVQA, a drug-protein interaction predictor that processes protein distance maps with a dynamic attentive CNN and drug SMILES strings with a self-attentive BiLSTM, then combines the representations for classification. It was benchmarked against established methods on three public datasets: Human, DUD-E, and BindingDB. On Human, comparisons included k-NN, random forest, L2-logistic, SVM, GNN, and a sequence-based DrugVQA variant. On DUD-E, comparisons included docking programs Vina and Smina, machine-learning scoring functions NN-Score and RF-Score, and deep learning methods 3D-CNN, AtomNet, and GNN. On BindingDB, comparisons included Tiresias, DBN, E2E, and GNN. This relates to the proposed NCGAMI benchmarking method because both use comparative evaluation of a new DTI predictor against established baselines, and the paper uses the Human dataset and AUC-style evaluation, although it does not use the proposed non-commutative, quantum, or domain-adaptation components and does not compare against DeepDTA, DeepConv-DTI, MolTrans, or TransformerCPI.
[results]
On the Human dataset, DrugVQA improved over the sequence-based variant by 1.9% in AUC, 3.3% in recall, and 4.4% in precision, while descriptor-based machine-learning baselines had AUC values ranging from 0.86 to 0.94. On DUD-E, DrugVQA achieved an average AUC of 0.971 compared with 0.94 for GNN, and it outperformed 3D-CNN on 96% of targets on a per-target basis. On BindingDB, DrugVQA was consistently better than E2E by 2.6% in average AUC and 2.3% in average accuracy, while Tiresias, DBN, and GNN performed better on seen proteins than on unseen proteins. No statistical significance tests were reported.
[context]
The model was implemented in PyTorch 0.4.0 and trained for at most 50 epochs using Adam with learning rate 0.001 and batch size 1 on a GTX1080Ti 12GB GPU. The CNN used 30 residual blocks with 16 and 32 filters, the BiLSTM hidden state was 64, dropout was 0.2, attention MLPs had 100 hidden units, protein matrix embeddings had 10 rows, drug matrix embeddings had 18 rows, and L2 regularization coefficient was 0.001. Evaluation used AUC for all datasets, precision and recall for Human, ROC enrichment at 0.5%, 1%, 2%, and 5% false positive rate thresholds for DUD-E, and accuracy for BindingDB. These conditions differ from the proposed plan, which specifies DrugBank and Human with 6:4 source-target splits, AUC and AUPR, ablations, theoretical validation, and eight A100 GPUs.
