[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Rendle et al., 2009-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/db16e908246f32b60a6e0a8e27093aa145fbb1ed)
- venue: Conference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 7095
[method]
The paper uses a leave-one-out AUC evaluation protocol for personalized ranking from implicit feedback. For each user, one observed user-item interaction is held out as the positive test item, while the model is trained on the remaining interactions. The model’s predicted ranking is then evaluated by comparing the held-out positive item against other items in the user’s test and training history, and the reported metric is the average AUC across users. This evaluation is applied to BPR-MF, BPR-kNN, WR-MF, SVD-MF, Cosine-kNN, most-popular, and a non-personalized upper bound. It relates to the proposed method because it uses AUC as a central performance measure, but it does not use AUPR and treats the task as pairwise ranking rather than binary classification with explicit precision-recall trade-offs.
[results]
The provided text does not report exact numerical AUC values. It states that the two BPR-optimized methods, BPR-MF and BPR-kNN, outperform all other methods on both the Rossmann online-shopping dataset and the Netflix video-rental dataset. Among matrix factorization models with the same architecture, BPR-MF clearly outperforms WR-MF and SVD-MF. SVD-MF is described as overfitting, with AUC decreasing as the number of dimensions increases, while WR-MF improves as dimensions increase. On Netflix, a BPR-MF model with 8 dimensions achieves AUC comparable to a WR-MF model with 128 dimensions. The results are presented graphically in the paper’s Figure 6, and no statistical significance tests are reported in the excerpt.
[context]
Experiments use two implicit-feedback datasets: Rossmann with 10,000 users, 4,000 items, and 426,612 purchases; and a Netflix subsample with 10,000 users, 5,000 items, and 565,738 rating actions, where every user and item has at least 10 interactions. The evaluation is repeated 10 times with new leave-one-out splits. Hyperparameters are selected by grid search in the first round and kept fixed for the remaining nine rounds. Factorization dimensions are varied from 8 to 128. The metric is average AUC, with 0.5 as the random-guess baseline and 1 as the ideal value; AUPR, precision, recall, or other classification-specific metrics are not reported.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Schrynemackers et al., 2013-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e752809d6ebaafdd5f040d763e536d3726416b9a)
- venue: Frontiers in Genetics
- citation count: 68
[method]
The paper uses an AUC/AUPR-based evaluation protocol for supervised network inference. It computes ROC and precision-recall curves from pair confidence scores, reports AUROC and AUPR, and separates predictions into four families according to whether the two nodes in a pair are present in the training pairs: LSxLS, LSxTS, TSxLS, and TSxTS. It compares pair-level cross-validation, where pairs are randomly held out, with node-level cross-validation, where nodes from each side of a bipartite graph or nodes in a homogeneous graph are held out. In the experiments, a local classifier based on Random Forests is trained in a 10-fold protocol on the DREAM5 in silico network, and performance is compared with a degree-based ranking baseline and with a control in which node features are randomly permuted. This is directly related to the proposed NCGAMI research plan because the plan also evaluates drug-target interaction prediction, a bipartite network inference task, using AUC and AUPR; the paper shows that these metrics should be interpreted with node-presence stratification and a degree baseline rather than as a single global score.
[results]
On the DREAM5 network, degree-based ranking alone obtained AUROC 0.83 and AUPR 0.14 for LSxLS pairs, AUROC 0.83 and AUPR 0.17 for LSxTS pairs, and AUROC 0.54 and AUPR 0.02 for TSxLS pairs. Random Forests obtained AUROC 0.85 and AUPR 0.31 for LSxLS pairs and AUROC 0.86 and AUPR 0.34 for LSxTS pairs, so AUROC was only slightly above the degree baseline while AUPR roughly doubled. With randomly permuted features, Random Forests still obtained AUROC 0.76 and AUPR 0.09 for LSxLS pairs and AUROC 0.78 and AUPR 0.11 for LSxTS pairs, and the per-transcription-factor averaged LSxTS performance was AUROC 0.48 and AUPR 0.02. In the four-family comparison, bipartite LSxTS performance was slightly better than LSxLS, while TSxLS and TSxTS were close to random; in the homogeneous setting, LSxLS was best, followed by LSxTS and then TSxTS. Per-node evaluation over 178 transcription factors showed that AUROC increased with node degree up to about 20 and then plateaued, whereas AUPR increased with degree across the observed range.
[context]
The experiments used the DREAM5 in silico network, with bipartite transcription factor-gene and homogeneous gene-gene settings, based on known regulatory networks from S. cerevisiae and E. coli. The network had a heavy-tailed degree distribution, with about 5 percent of transcription factors accounting for about 50 percent of interactions. The evaluation setup used 10-fold cross-validation, local Random Forest classifiers, ROC and precision-recall curves, AUROC and AUPR, degree-based ranking as a non-random baseline, and a permuted-feature control. Hardware, software versions, and detailed hyperparameters were not reported. This context matters for the proposed NCGAMI plan because drug-target interaction datasets can also have imbalanced and heavy-tailed target or compound degrees, and domain adaptation experiments can overstate performance if AUC/AUPR are computed only on known-known pairs or without a degree-based baseline.
[end related work 2]

