[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Peška et al., 2017-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/edb75d674b3cf94a396c8bdf856f855dee9ced06)
- venue: Comput. Methods Programs Biomed.
- citation count: 88
[method]
The paper uses a drug-target interaction prediction and evaluation method centered on BRDTI, a Bayesian personalized ranking matrix factorization model modified with content alignment and target bias. The model is trained on known drug-target interaction matrices and similarity features: drug-drug similarities from chemical structure via SIMCOMP for the GPCR, ion channel, nuclear receptor, and enzyme datasets, or 2D Tanimoto coefficients for the kinase dataset, and target-target similarities from normalized Smith-Waterman amino acid sequence scores. The evaluation protocol is five repetitions of 10-fold cross-validation, with known interactions randomly split into ten folds; each fold is held out as the test set while the remaining folds form the training set. Performance is reported as per-drug normalized discounted cumulative gain and AUC, with grid-search hyperparameter tuning and comparison against BLM-NII, WNN-GIP, NetLapRLS, and CMF. This relates to the proposed method because the paper explicitly uses AUC to assess DTI prediction performance, matching one of the proposed evaluation metrics, but it does not report AUPR and emphasizes ranking-based nDCG rather than precision-recall trade-off.
[results]
For AUC, BRDTI achieved the best result on all evaluated datasets except the kinase dataset, where it was second. It significantly outperformed the compared methods in 18 out of 20 dataset-metric cases for AUC using paired t-tests at p < 0.05. For nDCG, BRDTI achieved the best result on the GPCR, nuclear receptor, and enzyme datasets, and was second without significant difference on the ion channel and kinase datasets; it significantly outperformed competitors in 17 out of 20 nDCG cases. ROC curves on the GPCR dataset illustrated the AUC advantage, and similar ROC curves were reported for the other datasets.
[context]
The experimental setting involved five benchmark DTI datasets: GPCR, ion channel, nuclear receptor, enzyme, and kinase. The first four datasets provided binary drug-target interaction matrices; the kinase dataset contained continuous binding-affinity values that were binarized using a cutoff threshold. Evaluation was performed per drug and averaged over drugs and cross-validation runs. The paper did not specify hardware, software, or exact hyperparameter values in the provided text, only that grid search was used and details were in supplementary materials. A separate recently confirmed DTI dataset was constructed from current database profiles for validation, but the main reported outcomes were from the five benchmark datasets. The absence of AUPR and the use of nDCG mean the paper provides partial, not complete, support for the proposed AUC and AUPR evaluation plan.
[end related work 3]

