[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Wan et al., 2018-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9d6b11c830329622f251782f2736a56cd58c1ab2)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 339
[method]
The paper evaluated drug-target interaction prediction as a binary classification task using AUPR and AUROC, the latter corresponding to the proposed AUC metric. It constructed a heterogeneous network from eight relation types: drug-protein, drug-drug, protein-protein, drug-disease, protein-disease, drug-side-effect, drug chemical-structure similarity, and protein sequence similarity. Known drug-target pairs were positive examples and unknown pairs were negative examples. NeoDTI was trained by topology-preserving learning, with the DTI reconstruction loss computed only on training pairs while the other network reconstruction losses were computed normally. The evaluation used tenfold cross-validation with 90% training and 10% testing per fold, and compared NeoDTI against DTINet, HNM, NetLapRLS, DT-Hybrid, and BLMNII. Four evaluation scenarios were used: a 1:10 positive-to-negative sampling setting, an all-negative setting, redundancy-removal settings, and a unique-drug/target setting. Hyperparameters were selected using an independent validation set.
[results]
In the 1:10 positive-to-negative setting, NeoDTI achieved a reported significant improvement of 6.5% in AUPR and 2.7% in AUROC over the second-best baseline. In the all-negative setting, with a positive-to-negative ratio of about 1.8e-3, NeoDTI achieved a 27.6% AUPR improvement over the second-best method, while AUROC was comparable among NeoDTI, DTINet, and NetLapRLS. After removing redundant DTIs using similarity thresholds, all methods showed lower performance, but NeoDTI remained the best method on both AUPR and AUROC. In the unique DTI setting, where models were trained on non-unique interactions and tested on unique drugs or targets, NeoDTI outperformed all baselines by at least 13.3% in AUPR. The excerpt reports relative improvements rather than absolute AUPR or AUROC values, and does not provide p-values or confidence intervals.
[context]
The heterogeneous network was built from DrugBank version 3.0, HPRD release 9, CTDB, SIDER version 2, Morgan fingerprint-based drug structure similarity, and Smith-Waterman-based protein sequence similarity. Most interaction/association networks had binary edges, while the two similarity networks had non-negative real-valued edges. The main cross-validation used either ten times as many sampled negatives as positives or all unknown negatives, producing a highly imbalanced positive-to-negative ratio of about 1.8e-3 in the all-negative scenario. Redundancy tests removed DTIs with drug structure similarity above 0.6, protein sequence similarity above 40%, or Jaccard similarity above 0.6 for drug interactions, side effects, or diseases. The unique test required test drugs or targets to have only one known interaction partner. No hardware specifications, exact dataset sizes, or hyperparameter values were provided in the excerpt. This evaluation context is directly relevant to the proposed NCGAMI plan because it uses the same AUC/AUPR evaluation paradigm for DTI prediction and demonstrates that AUPR is especially informative for highly skewed drug-target interaction datasets.
[end related work 4]

