[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Liu, and Tsoumakas, 2021-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bda4faf04450e86b13d80cfa93c72476f19e6820)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 5
[method]
The paper uses AUC and AUPR as the evaluation metrics for drug-target interaction prediction, directly matching the proposed method’s plan to quantify classification and precision-recall performance. It applies these metrics to three matrix-factorization models: MFAUPR, which optimizes a surrogate AUPR loss; MFAUC, which optimizes a surrogate AUC loss; and MF2A, which ensembles the two to optimize both metrics. Before prediction, multiple drug and target similarities are fused using local interaction consistency. The evaluation is conducted on updated golden-standard DTI datasets for nuclear receptors, GPCRs, ion channels, and enzymes, and on Luo’s larger DTI dataset. Four cross-validation settings are used: S1 10-fold pair CV, S2 10-fold drug CV, S3 10-fold target CV, and S4 3x3 block-wise CV. The paper compares the proposed methods against fifteen baselines, including neighborhood, tree, matrix-factorization, kernel, and network-based methods, and reports significance using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Bergman-Hommel correction at the 5% level across prediction settings. This relates to the NCGAMI research plan because AUC and AUPR are the planned metrics for assessing the quantum-classical DTI framework, and the paper shows how these metrics can reveal both overall ranking quality and precision-recall behavior under imbalanced DTI data.
[results]
Across the golden-standard datasets, MF2A is the best method in all prediction settings and significantly outperforms all competitors, including MFAUPR and MFAUC, in both AUC and AUPR. MFAUPR and MFAUC are second-best only behind MF2A in the metric they optimize and achieve significant wins over the other comparable methods in that metric, but their performance in the other metric is weaker, with the paper reporting fifth-place performance and inferiority to two competitors in the non-target metric. On Luo’s dataset in S1, MF2A, MFAUPR, and MFAUC are the top three AUPR methods, improving over the best competitor NeoDTI by 21.2%, 17.3%, and 11.3%, respectively. For AUC, MF2A is second best, 1.5% lower than DTIP, while MFAUC and MFAUPR outperform the second-best competitor DTINet by 4.8% and 0.4%, respectively. In the similarity-integration comparison on S4, local interaction consistency is best in most cases, followed by kernel alignment and HSIC; HSIC is best on the small NR dataset, while AVE and SNF are worst, with SNF usually inferior to AVE. Convergence curves show objective values dropping sharply in the first 10 iterations and then stabilizing, and the proposed decay-coefficient selection strategy matches grid-search performance with more than 9.5x speed-up in S2, S3, and S4.
[context]
Dataset characteristics are NR: 54 drugs, 26 targets, 166 interactions, sparsity 0.118; GPCR: 223 drugs, 95 targets, 1096 interactions, sparsity 0.052; IC: 210 drugs, 204 targets, 2331 interactions, sparsity 0.054; E: 445 drugs, 664 targets, 4256 interactions, sparsity 0.014; Luo: 708 drugs, 1512 targets, 1923 interactions, sparsity 0.002. The golden-standard datasets use four drug similarities and four target similarities selected for high local interaction consistency; Luo’s dataset uses four drug similarities and three target similarities. Methods that accept only one similarity are given the average of the corresponding multiple similarities. Key hyperparameters include k=5, learning rate 0.1, candidate decay coefficients from 0.1 to 1.0, 100 iterations, embedding dimension r in {50,100}, regularization coefficients in {2^-4, 2^-2, 2^0, 2^2}, number of bins in {11,16,21,26,31}, and ensemble weight beta from 0 to 1 in steps of 0.01. Network-based baselines are evaluated only on Luo’s S1 because they generally cannot handle new-drug or new-target settings. Hardware specifications are not reported in the provided text. These conditions are relevant to the NCGAMI plan, which also uses DTI datasets, domain partitioning, and AUC/AUPR evaluation, and therefore benefits from the paper’s demonstration that AUPR and AUC can expose different model strengths under imbalanced interaction prediction.
[end related work 5]

