[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Shtar et al., 2023-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8410f2619ad44475c479d7e23049740ed92d288b)
- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 15
[method]
The paper uses an evaluation-metrics approach for molecular interaction prediction, directly related to the proposed method because it also uses AUC and AUPR to quantify classification and precision-recall performance. The evaluated model is LAMFP, a lookup-based extension of AMFP that predicts drug-drug interactions for unseen drugs by transferring interaction evidence from known drugs using molecular similarity measures, including Tanimoto, ED, NLCS, TF, and an ensemble of these measures. The method ranks candidate drug pairs and evaluates them with AUC as the primary classification metric, AUPR for precision-recall behavior under class imbalance, and additionally MRR, MAP, accuracy, specificity, and sensitivity. Inputs are DrugBank 5.1.3 as the training version and DrugBank 5.1.6 as the test version, restricted to drugs with SMILES representations and at least one interaction, with test subsets for known-new, new-new, and all interactions.
[results]
Using AUC and AUPR, LAMFP achieved the highest AUC and MRR across all test subsets. A Friedman test on AUC values gave P < 0.002, and pairwise comparisons of LAMFP against other methods gave P < 1e-5. For LAMFP similarity variants, the ensemble of similarity measures gave the best AUC and AUPR: AUC 0.815, MRR 0.541, AUPR 0.338 on all interactions; AUC 0.819, MRR 0.541, AUPR 0.346 on known-new interactions; and AUC 0.740, MRR 0.310, AUPR 0.206 on new-new interactions. Tanimoto was close, with AUC 0.809, 0.814, and 0.733, and AUPR 0.338, 0.348, and 0.191 for all, known-new, and new-new subsets, respectively. ED, NLCS, and TF were lower, with AUPR values ranging from 0.130 to 0.313 across subsets. LAMFP also showed a balanced combination of accuracy, specificity, and sensitivity: accuracy 0.8249, specificity 0.8614, and sensitivity 0.5457 on all interactions; accuracy 0.8216, specificity 0.8574, and sensitivity 0.5574 on known-new interactions; and accuracy 0.8608, specificity 0.9024, and sensitivity 0.3462 on new-new interactions.
[context]
The experimental setting is a retrospective drug-drug interaction prediction task, not drug-target interaction prediction, and it does not include quantum, non-commutative, or domain-adaptation components. DrugBank 5.1.3 contained 3,478 drugs, 1,237,987 positive interactions, and 5,383,863 negative interactions; DrugBank 5.1.6 added 843 drugs, 221,449 positive interactions, and 2,490,061 negative interactions. After preprocessing, the final training and test sets contained 2,847 and 530 drugs, respectively. Absent interactions were treated as true negatives, following common link-prediction and recommender-system practice. Hardware, hyperparameters, and implementation details for LAMFP are not specified in the provided sections. The relation to the proposed research plan is primarily at the evaluation level: both use AUC and AUPR to assess binary interaction prediction and precision-recall trade-offs, but the paper's AUPR values are modest and specific to DDI prediction with a large number of negative pairs.
[end related work 6]

