- in_text_citation: [(Rayhan et al., 2018-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d451059d9099e01ecff5786750731ecc1145f3fd)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 5
[method]
The paper evaluates a two-stage drug-target interaction prediction pipeline in which FRnet-1, a convolutional deep autoencoder with max-pooling and fully connected layers, reconstructs structural and evolutionary drug-target feature inputs and produces 4096 features from its first fully connected layer. FRnet-2 is then used as the classifier, and the same 4096 features are also fed to alternative classifiers, including decision tree, SVM, MEBoost, and CUSBoost, to compare downstream module choices. This module-substitution design is related to the proposed NCGAMI ablation plan because it tests alternative architectures for a key downstream component while holding the upstream feature extractor fixed, although it does not remove a domain adaptation component. Experiments used four imbalanced gold-standard DTI datasets, namely Enzyme, GPCR, Ion Channel, and Nuclear Receptor, with 5-fold shuffled cross-validation, 10 repeated runs, and averaged auPR and auROC evaluation.
[results]
Using 4096 FRnet-1 features, FRnet-2 achieved auPR/auROC of 0.70/0.9754 on Enzyme, 0.69/0.9512 on GPCR, 0.49/0.9478 on Ion Channel, and 0.73/0.9241 on Nuclear Receptor. It outperformed CUSBoost in auROC on all four datasets, with CUSBoost values of 0.9345, 0.8989, 0.8851, and 0.8989, respectively. FRnet-2 also outperformed CUSBoost in auPR on GPCR, Ion Channel, and Nuclear Receptor, with CUSBoost auPR values of 0.65, 0.45, and 0.71, respectively, while CUSBoost had a slightly higher Enzyme auPR of 0.71 compared with 0.70 for FRnet-2. FRnet-2 outperformed decision tree, SVM, and MEBoost in both auPR and auROC on all four datasets; for example, on Enzyme it exceeded SVM 0.53/0.9010, MEBoost 0.41/0.9404, and decision tree 0.28/0.9376. The paper reports no statistical significance tests.
[context]
Dataset sizes were Enzyme 445 drugs, 664 proteins, 2926 positive interactions, and imbalance ratio 99.98; Ion Channel 210 drugs, 204 proteins, 1476 positive interactions, and imbalance ratio 28.02; GPCR 223 drugs, 95 proteins, 635 positive interactions, and imbalance ratio 32.36; Nuclear Receptor 54 drugs, 26 proteins, 90 positive interactions, and imbalance ratio 14.6. FRnet-1 was trained per dataset to reconstruct inputs, reached over 95% reconstruction accuracy, and was capped below 97% by aggressive regularization, using dropout keep probability 0.5, L2 regularization in each layer, and learning rate 0.001. The feature input shape is reported as [X, 7, 211, 1]. Implementation used Python 3.6, TensorFlow, and scikit-learn, but hardware specifications were not provided. Evaluation emphasized auPR and auROC because of class imbalance, with 5-fold shuffled cross-validation and 10 repeated experiments averaged.
