- in_text_citation: [(Lei et al., 2022-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/56d731ffa9c6b9edf4a1d18b49e76ef5d08a4c8a)
- venue: Frontiers in Pharmacology
- citation count: 11
[method]
The paper performed component-ablation experiments for the VGAEDR drug-disease association prediction model. VGAEDR learns representations from a drug-disease heterogeneous network using a variational graph autoencoder ensemble followed by a CNN-based feature dimension reduction module and a fully connected softmax classifier. The heterogeneous network was built from known drug-disease associations, disease semantic similarity, and drug similarity networks derived from chemical substructure, target protein domains, and target gene annotations. For ablation, the authors trained variants with different subsets of the three drug attribute inputs, denoted Che, Dom, and Anno, including the full model and reduced-feature variants, with the worst-performing variant lacking Che. They also replaced the VGAE graph encoder with GCN or GAT while keeping the downstream CNN and classification layers, creating GCN_DR and GAT_DR variants. This is methodologically aligned with the proposed NCGAMI ablation plan, which removes the UDA component or replaces key modules, because both use leave-one-component-out or substitute-module experiments to test whether each part contributes to predictive performance in a graph-based biomedical interaction prediction framework.
[results]
The full model using Che, Dom, and Anno achieved the best AUC and AUPR. The Che+Dom variant had AUC and AUPR 0.0325 and 0.0341 lower than the full model, respectively. The Che+Anno variant had AUC and AUPR 0.0118 and 0.031 lower than the full model, respectively. The variant without Che achieved the lowest AUC and AUPR, indicating that chemical substructure information contributed most strongly, while target protein domain and gene annotation information had smaller but still positive effects. In the graph-encoder replacement experiment on Dataset1, VGAEDR outperformed GCN_DR and GAT_DR, supporting the choice of VGAE over GCN and GAT for learning the drug-disease heterogeneous network; the text did not provide exact AUC or AUPR values for the GCN_DR and GAT_DR variants.
[context]
The experiments were conducted on drug-disease association datasets rather than drug-target interaction or domain-adaptation datasets. The main CTD-derived dataset contained 855 drugs, 727 diseases, and 29,274 associations after filtering to drugs and diseases with more than 10 associations. Benchmark Dataset1 contained 269 drugs, 598 diseases, and 18,416 associations, and Dataset2 contained 763 drugs, 681 diseases, and 3,051 associations. Positive samples were known associations; negative samples were randomly paired unobserved drug-disease pairs equal in number to positives. Evaluation used 5-fold cross-validation, with four folds used for pretraining or parameter analysis and one independent test fold, repeated five times and averaged. Metrics included AUC, AUPR, F1 score, accuracy, specificity, precision, and recall. Batch-size sensitivity analysis on the CTD dataset found batch size 128 to be optimal. Hardware specifications were not reported. The paper does not include unsupervised domain adaptation, non-commutative geometry, or quantum adiabatic optimization, so its relevance to the proposed NCGAMI plan is primarily as a comparable ablation and module-replacement protocol for graph-based interaction prediction.
