- in_text_citation: [(Abbasi et al., 2020-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ad671537693f3d2f2a0b19d7a48157d772cb7bb3)
- venue: Bioinform.
- citation count: 271
[method]
The paper empirically validates a drug-target interaction prediction model that combines convolutional and LSTM feature encoders, two-sided attention, and adversarial domain adaptation. This is related to the proposed NCGAMI validation method because it tests a domain-adaptation component for drug-target interaction prediction on standard benchmarks, although it uses classical adversarial adaptation rather than a spectral-action, non-commutative, or quantum adiabatic formulation. The experiments use the KIBA, Davis, and BindingDB datasets. For KIBA and Davis, nested cross-validation is used: the dataset is split into six equal parts, one part is held out as the test set, and the remaining five parts are used for 5-fold cross-validation; reported values are averages of five runs. The same train/test split is used for baseline comparisons. For BindingDB, a fixed train/test partition is used, with four protein classes removed from training to test generalization. Baselines include KronRLS, SimBoost, DeepAffinity, and DeepDTA. Ablations compare the full model with an LSTM-only encoder and a version without attention. Evaluation uses continuous metrics including the Concordance Index, mean squared error, r2m, and Pearson correlation, plus binary AUPR after thresholding predictions; thresholds are 7 for Davis, 12.1 for KIBA, and 7.6 for BindingDB.
[results]
On KIBA, the full model outperforms all baselines on all reported metrics, with a 0.026 higher Concordance Index, an MSE 0.018 lower than DeepDTA, and about 0.024 higher AUPR than the best comparable baseline; attention improves the Concordance Index by 0.014 relative to the no-attention variant, and a paired t-test against DeepDTA gives p less than 0.0001. On Davis, the full model reports improvements over DeepDTA of 0.013 in Concordance Index, 0.019 in AUPR, and 0.025 in r2m, with a reported r2m value of 0.248; the LSTM-only variant is 0.014 lower in Concordance Index than DeepDTA, while the attention-included ablation reports a 0.005 Concordance Index advantage over DeepDTA; a paired t-test against DeepDTA gives p around 0.001. On BindingDB, the full model achieves a higher Pearson correlation coefficient than DeepAffinity and DeepDTA, the CNN-LSTM encoder outperforms CNN-only and LSTM-only encoders, and domain adaptation improves all metrics over the no-attention variant; a paired t-test against DeepDTA gives p around 0.01.
[context]
Dataset sizes are: KIBA originally contains 467 proteins, 52,498 compounds, and 246,088 interactions, filtered to proteins and compounds with at least 10 interactions; Davis contains 442 proteins and 68 compounds with Kd values transformed to log space; BindingDB uses Ki-labelled samples and excludes nuclear estrogen receptors with 516 samples, ion channels with 8,101 samples, receptor tyrosine kinases with 3,355 samples, and G-protein-coupled receptors with 77,994 samples from training, leaving 101,134 training and 43,391 test samples. The datasets are imbalanced with ratios of 0.082, 0.192, and 0.349, which motivates the use of AUPR. Hyperparameters include filter counts searched over 16, 32, 64, 128, and 256 for proteins and compounds, compound and protein filter lengths, feature-encoder learning rates over 0.1, 0.01, 0.001, and 0.0001, test feature-encoder learning rates over 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, and 0.00001, and discriminator learning rates over 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, and 0.00001. Computation uses an Intel Core i7-7700HQ CPU, an NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1070 8GB GPU, 32GB DDR4 RAM, Python 3.6, TensorFlow, Keras, a minibatch size of 256, and about 0.01 second per iteration. This context is relevant to the proposed NCGAMI validation because it defines a classical drug-target interaction and domain-adaptation benchmarking protocol, but it does not include non-commutative spectral geometry, fluctuation-theorem tests, or quantum adiabatic convergence checks.
[end related work 4]

