[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Rajbhandari et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/00c957711b12468cb38424caccdf5291bb354033)
- venue: International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis
- citation count: 2265
[method]
The paper implements and evaluates ZeRO-100B, a PyTorch-based distributed training system for large transformer models. It combines ZeRO-DP partitioning of optimizer states and gradients with ZeRO-R partitioned activation checkpointing and optional CPU offload, and can be combined with Megatron-LM model parallelism. The implementation wraps torch.nn.Module, uses data-parallel communication primitives such as all-reduce, reduce-scatter, and all-gather, and analyzes memory and communication volume for model states and activations. This is related to the proposed NCGAMI computational implementation because both rely on distributed GPU training, but the paper's method is specifically a memory-partitioning and communication-aware training system rather than a drug-target interaction prediction algorithm.
[results]
ZeRO-100B enabled training of models up to 170B parameters, reported as 8x larger than the state-of-the-art at the time, with up to 10x faster training and improved usability. For Turing-NLG with 17B parameters, it achieved a Webtext-103 perplexity of 10.21 and sustained throughput of 41.4 TFlops/GPU over 300K iterations. With fixed batch size and model parallelism degree 16, maximum trainable model size increased from 40B to 60B when partitioned activation checkpointing was enabled, to 140B when optimizer-state and gradient partitioning was enabled, and to 150B when partitioned activation checkpoints were offloaded to CPU. Communication analysis showed that optimizer-state and gradient partitioning added no additional communication relative to baseline data parallelism, while parameter partitioning increased communication by at most 1.5x; partitioned activation checkpointing added less than 10% communication overhead relative to model-parallel communication.
[context]
Experiments used 400 V100 GPUs across 25 DGX-2 nodes with 800 Gbps internode bandwidth, model parallelism degree 16, and fixed batch size. Baselines were PyTorch distributed data parallel for non-model-parallel settings and Megatron-LM for model-parallel settings. The evaluated models were transformer language models, not drug-target interaction datasets, and the hardware was V100 rather than the proposed eight A100 40GB GPUs. The method is therefore relevant as a distributed training and memory-management approach that could support large-scale training or tuning of the NCGAMI framework if model states or activations exceed single-GPU memory, but it does not directly validate the non-commutative geometric, quantum adiabatic, or DTI-specific components of the research plan.
[end related work 1]

