[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Zhao et al., 2024-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/40e996a7c3e914a67c708704fa9b4c54ea70f36e)
- venue: AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 152
[method]
The paper's implementation details describe a distributed supervised fine-tuning setup for Mamba-based multimodal large language models, which is related to the proposed method's plan to use eight A100 GPUs and tuned hyperparameters for large-scale training. The approach uses PyTorch Fully Sharded Data Parallel and automatic mixed precision with FP32/BF16 on eight NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs. Inputs include images resized to 384x384 and normalized with backbone defaults, patch features from the penultimate vision encoder layer, and text tokens from a GPT-NeoX-style tokenizer; image embeddings are concatenated to the beginning of the text embedding sequence before Mamba generation. Training uses supervised fine-tuning for two epochs with batch size 128, AdamW, cosine-decay learning rate 2e-5, decay factor 0.1, and warm-up ratio 0.03. Evaluation uses a forked vlm-evaluation tool on six MLLM benchmarks, localization benchmarks, and TextVQA, with accuracy-based metrics such as VQA accuracy, POPE, VSR, and localization accuracy. This is relevant to the NCGAMI research plan because it provides a concrete 8-GPU A100 distributed training and hyperparameter template, but it is from an MLLM domain and uses 80GB GPUs rather than the proposed 40GB GPUs, and it does not report regularization parameters.
[results]
Using this setup, training a 2.8B-parameter model took about 26.5 hours on eight A100 80GB GPUs. The resulting Cobra-8B model achieved the best performance on all six evaluated benchmarks among large-scale MLLMs with more than 7B parameters. Cobra-3.5B achieved the best performance on all benchmarks except VQA-v2 and GQA, where MoELLAVA was 1%-2% more accurate, while Cobra's inference speed was over 8 times faster. Compared with LLaVA v1.5-7B, Cobra-3.5B used only 48% of the total parameters, achieved comparable open-ended VQA results, and improved VSR and POPE by 6.9% and 2.5%, respectively. For localization, Cobra-3.5B was 3%-4% lower than LLaVA v1.5-7B across three benchmarks, whereas Cobra-8B had the highest accuracy and outperformed the other two models by over 3% on all three. On TextVQA, placing OCR tokens before the question improved accuracy by over 10% for all models; in OCR-last mode, most models performed lower than without OCR tokens except two variants.
[context]
Key conditions include eight NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs, PyTorch FSDP, automatic mixed precision with FP32/BF16, batch size 128, AdamW optimizer, cosine-decay learning rate 2e-5, decay factor 0.1, warm-up ratio 0.03, and two supervised fine-tuning epochs. The 2.8B Mamba backbone was pretrained on 627B tokens from SlimPajama, with additional chat and preference tuning variants; the 7B backbone was pretrained on 1.2T tokens from RefinedWeb and not chat fine-tuned. Images were processed at 384x384 using torchvision/TIMM defaults, and evaluation was performed with a forked vlm-evaluation tool. The paper does not report regularization parameters, exact dataset splits for the MLLM benchmarks, or training time for the 7B model, so the direct transferability to the proposed eight 40GB A100 setup for NCGAMI is limited.
[end related work 3]

