[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Wulff et al., 2024-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/91f0a1ba45fb3fbb300f5c7fb35c269a3f7b9466)
- venue: Quantum Machine Intelligence
- citation count: 7
[method]
The paper implements a distributed hybrid quantum-classical hyperparameter optimization workflow based on Swift-Hyperband. Swift-Hyperband extends Hyperband by adding one performance-prediction decision point per bracket round, using early training curves to predict later validation loss or accuracy and terminate unpromising trials earlier. The performance predictors are support vector regression models, including a classical SVR baseline and a quantum support vector regression variant trained on a D-Wave Advantage quantum annealer. The QT-SVR formulation encodes the SVR training problem as a QUBO problem and combines multiple heuristically obtained quantum predictions into a single prediction. The workflow uses MPI to coordinate a head node and up to 50 GPU-equipped worker nodes on an HPC cluster, while the head node communicates with the quantum annealer through the D-Wave Ocean SDK. Target models include fully connected neural networks on OpenML tabular regression datasets, convolutional neural networks on CIFAR-10 and TinyImageNet, an LSTM on bAbI task 17, and an MLPF particle-flow model on the DELPHES high-energy physics dataset. Tuned hyperparameters include learning rate, batch size, weight decay or momentum, and architecture parameters such as depth, width, number of convolutional layers, and number of filters. This method is relevant to the proposed research plan because it demonstrates a distributed GPU-based hyperparameter tuning workflow with performance prediction, which is directly analogous to the proposed use of eight A100 GPUs and tuned learning rates, batch sizes, and regularization parameters. However, the paper does not address drug-target interaction prediction, non-commutative geometry, spectral action, or quantum adiabatic optimization, so its relevance is primarily to distributed training, hyperparameter search, and hybrid quantum-classical computation rather than to the scientific model itself.
[results]
Swift-Hyperband with both classical SVR and QT-SVR achieved target-model performance similar to plain Hyperband while consuming fewer total training epochs in all reported cases. The largest resource saving was approximately 9.4% on CIFAR-10, where Hyperband used 3237 epochs, Swift-Hyperband with classical SVR used 3014 epochs, and Swift-Hyperband with QT-SVR used 2960 epochs. On TinyImageNet, Hyperband required 872 training iterations, while Swift-Hyperband with QT-SVR required 834 iterations, a saving of approximately 4.6%, with validation loss of 0.014 for QT-SVR compared with 0.012 for Hyperband. For the remaining cases, savings were approximately 2% to 5%. QT-SVR matched or outperformed classical SVR in the majority of cases in terms of final validation performance. QT-SVR used fewer epochs than classical SVR for the CNN cases but more epochs for the LSTM, MLPF, and fully connected network cases, indicating that the quantum predictor sometimes overestimated or underestimated future performance depending on the learning-curve shape. On the Grid Stability dataset, a D-Wave hybrid solver outperformed both SVR-based and QT-SVR-based Swift-Hyperband in best-found model quality and number of epochs used, while plain Hyperband achieved the lowest overall loss but at substantially higher computational cost.
[context]
Experiments were run on 51 nodes of the DEEP-EST supercomputer together with the JUPSI D-Wave Advantage quantum annealer. The distributed implementation used one head node to coordinate up to 50 GPU worker nodes and to communicate with the quantum hardware. Results were averaged over three random seeds, 0, 1, and 2. Maximum training epochs were 50 for the OpenML fully connected network cases, 100 for CIFAR-10, 35 for TinyImageNet, 300 for the bAbI LSTM case, and 100 for the MLPF high-energy physics case. Evaluation metrics were best validation accuracy or loss and average total training epochs consumed. The paper notes that current quantum hardware limits problem size, and that the QT-SVR approach is intended to become more advantageous for larger datasets on future quantum hardware. The method is contextually useful for the proposed eight-A100 implementation because it shows how distributed GPU training can be combined with early-stopping and performance prediction to reduce hyperparameter search cost, but it does not provide evidence for the proposed non-commutative geometric or adiabatic quantum optimization components.
[end related work 4]

