[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Crane et al., 2011-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6787629238b5c20ceeff65abdea6924c1d653d1c)
- venue: ACM Transactions on Graphics
- citation count: 105
[method]
The paper uses a discrete Dirac operator on triangle meshes with boundary, where boundary conditions are imposed by prescribing initial and target unit tangent directions at boundary vertices rather than fixing boundary point positions. At each boundary vertex, two real parameters are stored to define a local boundary block that rotates from the initial tangent to the target tangent and then applies scaling and rotation around the tangent; in the planar target case this corresponds to prescribing boundary curvature. The validation method compares the eigenvalues of the discrete operator with the known spectrum of the smooth Dirac operator and tests discretization behavior by applying a bump to a flat disk on uniform and nonuniform samplings. This relates to the proposed research method because the proposed NCGAMI interpretability analysis also relies on spectral properties of a Dirac operator, although the paper provides a geometric validation template rather than a drug-target interaction method.
[results]
On a coarse unit-sphere mesh with 8k triangles, the first 20 distinct eigenvalues out of 120 total from the discrete operator closely matched the predicted smooth spectrum; the paper describes the match as exceptionally well but does not report numerical error, confidence intervals, or statistical significance. A boundary-modification example reports a mean Q value of 1.016, indicating that boundary tangent prescription can alter the boundary without large distortion. Uniform and nonuniform sampling of a flat disk with an added bump produced similar results, supporting discretization stability, but no baseline comparison, convergence rate, or biological relevance metric is provided in the excerpt.
[context]
The experiments are geometric validation studies on synthetic surfaces, not molecular or drug-target data. Reported conditions include an 8k-triangle unit sphere, 120 total eigenvalues with 20 distinct values plotted, prescribed boundary tangent directions, planar target boundary curvature in the planar case, and uniform versus nonuniform domain sampling. Hardware, software, hyperparameters, and biological evaluation metrics are not reported. This context is important for the proposed research plan because it shows that discrete Dirac spectra can be validated against smooth theoretical references, but it does not demonstrate that such spectral features are biologically meaningful for drug-target interaction prediction or that they transfer to non-commutative pharmacological manifolds.
[end related work 1]

